{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And may the Elohim give you.</b> The reason he said it with a vav at the beginning of the matter is that, having preceded and said ״וַיְבָרְכֵהוּ״, he further added to say ״וְיִתֶּן לְךָ״ etc. And as for why he did not specify the blessings alluded to in ״וַיְבָרְכֵהוּ״ — perhaps he intends along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory, in Bereishit Rabbah (39:11): that the master key of blessings HaShem gave to Abraham, and Abraham to Isaac (Bereishit Rabbah 11), and now Isaac handed them to Jacob. And that is what he said, <b>וַיְבָרְכֵהוּ</b> — meaning, he handed him the blessings so that they would be in his domain." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that he blessed him with spiritual blessings and material blessings. Corresponding to the spiritual ones he said in concealed fashion ״וַיְבָרְכֵהוּ״, and corresponding to the material ones he said <b>וְיִתֶּן לְךָ</b> etc., with the addition of the vav, to say: besides the supernal world which Abraham chose for his descendants — which is the supreme purpose of the seed of Israel — He will further give him in this world, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend along this line: not only the attribute of mercy, but also the attribute of judgment will concur with this giving. And that is what he said, <b>וְיִתֶּן לְךָ הָאֱלֹהִים</b> — meaning, also the attribute of Elohim."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The citation 'Bereishit Rabbah 11' appears without a chapter number; rendered as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:55.355200096Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}